# Testing Rule — Goblin Cannon

## When to Run Tests

Run the full test suite **after completing any major feature work, bug fix, or significant refactor**. This includes:

- Adding or removing game systems
- Changing energy routing, combat, rewards, or game state logic
- Modifying ball definitions or reward candidates
- Changing milestone/timer/wall mechanics
- Any structural code change across multiple files

## How to Run Tests

Execute from the project root:

```
godot --headless -s tests/run_tests.gd
```

The runner executes scripts listed in `tests/run_tests.gd` (`TEST_SCRIPTS`), runs them, and exits with code 0 (all pass) or 1 (any failure). Add new suites to that array.

## After Running Tests

- If any tests fail, **fix the code or update the tests** before considering the work complete.
- If a feature changes existing behavior, **update the relevant tests** to match the new expectations.
- If a feature adds new systems, **add new test files** following the `tests/test_<system>.gd` convention.

## Test File Conventions

- Each test file extends `TestBase` (from `tests/test_base.gd`)
- Set `suite_name` in `_init()`
- Implement `run()` which calls individual `test_*()` methods
- Use `begin("description")` before assertions for clear error messages
- Use `assert_eq`, `assert_true`, `assert_false`, `assert_gt`, `assert_lt`, `assert_in`, `assert_not_in`, `assert_empty`, `assert_not_empty`, `assert_approx`
- For Node-based classes: instantiate via `Node.new()` + `set_script(load(...))` and call methods directly
- For classes needing GameState: call `GameState.start_run(seed)` first to ensure clean state

